One thing worth flagging, since it changed the implementation: a straightforward expression-for-expression translation was already bit-exact for all 7.9M non-NaN inputs, but differed on 7,521 NaN cases. The cause was codegen, not logic — when both operands of an SSE op are NaN, the result is the [3mdestination[23m operand's NaN, and LLVM rewrote [38;5;10ml - 0.5*c[0m into [38;5;10m(-0.5)*c + l[0m, flipping which NaN's sign bit survived. I replaced the bare operators with [38;5;10madd[0m/[38;5;10msub[0m/[38;5;10mmul[0m/[38;5;10mdiv[0m helpers whose first argument is the destination operand, with roles read off the compiled C. For inputs where at most one operand is NaN these are plain IEEE arithmetic, so nothing else is affected. Note this pins NaN sign/payload behavior to GCC's codegen for this machine; that aspect is inherently compiler-dependent, whereas the non-NaN equivalence is not.[0m[0m
